1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,199
You have a legal right to delete your
data.

2
00:00:02,199 --> 00:00:03,866
Almost nobody finishes.

3
00:00:03,866 --> 00:00:04,479
The

4
00:00:04,479 --> 00:00:09,514
controls are scattered, the retention
rules are buried, and the last button is

5
00:00:09,514 --> 00:00:10,556
irreversible.

6
00:00:10,556 --> 00:00:14,560
So you either click through a dozen
screens hoping you understood them, or you

7
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:18,102
ask a chatbot that can't see your account
and can only guess.

8
00:00:18,102 --> 00:00:19,468
Neither one shows

9
00:00:19,468 --> 00:00:21,007
you what you're about to lose.

10
00:00:21,007 --> 00:00:23,565
This is the Personal Data Rights
Workbench.

11
00:00:23,565 --> 00:00:26,134
The page itself hands the agent nine

12
00:00:26,134 --> 00:00:32,338
WebMCP tools: inventory, consent,
retention rules, planning, staging,

13
00:00:32,338 --> 00:00:34,306
cancellation, and a receipt.

14
00:00:34,306 --> 00:00:37,697
The same data I can see — structured so an
agent

15
00:00:37,697 --> 00:00:39,201
can actually reason about it.

16
00:00:39,201 --> 00:00:41,863
One sentence, in a fresh Codex session.

17
00:00:41,863 --> 00:00:44,398
Keep the tax invoices and the delivery

18
00:00:44,398 --> 00:00:49,896
addresses, drop location history and
advertising profiles, opt out of sale or

19
00:00:49,896 --> 00:00:52,072
sharing, and stage it for review.

20
00:00:52,072 --> 00:00:54,456
Watch what happens before anything is
touched.

21
00:00:54,456 --> 00:00:56,968
It reads the fourteen categories,

22
00:00:56,968 --> 00:00:58,947
the four consent switches, and the

23
00:00:58,947 --> 00:01:01,609
retention rules — and it finds two
categories

24
00:01:01,609 --> 00:01:05,185
it isn't allowed to delete yet, so it
never promises to delete them.

25
00:01:05,185 --> 00:01:09,144
Then the plan arrives, and the page
rearranges itself around it.

26
00:01:09,144 --> 00:01:10,475
Nine categories

27
00:01:10,475 --> 00:01:14,444
to delete, two held back by rule, three
kept because I asked.

28
00:01:14,444 --> 00:01:15,694
Almost the entire

29
00:01:15,694 --> 00:01:16,806
footprint goes.

30
00:01:16,806 --> 00:01:20,139
But the part that really matters is the
trade-off, in plain

31
00:01:20,139 --> 00:01:25,081
language: this account stops learning the
places I go, and my ads become

32
00:01:25,081 --> 00:01:27,025
contextual instead of profiled.

33
00:01:27,025 --> 00:01:32,708
Every category has to be explicitly
deleted or kept — silence never becomes

34
00:01:32,708 --> 00:01:34,132
consent on my behalf.

35
00:01:34,132 --> 00:01:37,789
And each staged action is bound to this
exact plan

36
00:01:37,789 --> 00:01:42,176
version, so a stale or half-remembered
plan is simply rejected.

37
00:01:42,176 --> 00:01:44,144
Now the part I care about most.

38
00:01:44,144 --> 00:01:45,486
There is no commit tool.

39
00:01:45,486 --> 00:01:47,083
The agent can inspect,

40
00:01:47,083 --> 00:01:50,544
plan, explain, and stage — it cannot pull
the trigger.

41
00:01:50,544 --> 00:01:51,910
That step stays with me.

42
00:01:51,910 --> 00:01:57,616
And the state really moves: nine records
erased, sale or sharing off, staging

43
00:01:57,616 --> 00:01:59,468
cleared, receipt issued.

44
00:01:59,468 --> 00:02:02,581
When the agent reads that receipt back, it
matches the

45
00:02:02,581 --> 00:02:03,692
page exactly.

46
00:02:03,692 --> 00:02:06,701
Nothing invented, nothing quietly assumed.

47
00:02:06,701 --> 00:02:09,838
Agents shouldn't just click through your
privacy settings faster.

48
00:02:09,838 --> 00:02:10,648
They should

49
00:02:10,648 --> 00:02:13,414
make consequences legible before you
commit.

50
00:02:13,414 --> 00:02:15,984
That's what WebMCP makes possible

51
00:02:15,984 --> 00:02:20,486
here — on synthetic data, with the final
say still mine.
